{
  "statement": "Raw compute thresholds cannot replace the institutional machinery that governs software deployment because hardware metrics ignore how courts, agencies, and insurers actually evaluate liability. When the General Services Administration procures enterprise software, contracting officers do not audit floating-point operations per second; they examine indemnification clauses, service-level agreements, and documented error rates under operational stress. Tying governance milestones directly to training compute ignores the base rate of how every general-purpose technology has been absorbed into the economy. Financial software and medical devices did not scale safely because regulators counted transistors or watts; they cleared markets because liability rules and tort law forced developers to prove fault resistance in domain-specific use cases.\n\nTo understand why hardware caps fail as a regulatory instrument, examine the reference class of aviation software certification. The Federal Aviation Administration regulates flight control systems not by capping the clock speed of the onboard computer, but by demanding rigorous failure-mode testing and proof that a software glitch will not cascade into catastrophic physical harm. Insurers underwrite risk based on historical loss data and verifiable procedural controls, not on the training budget of the underlying model. When an algorithm fails in a municipal deployment or a commercial bank, the resulting litigation targets the entity that certified the deployment for a specific task, not the cluster size used during pre-training. A compute-scaling rule leaves the actual mechanism of institutional accountability completely unaddressed.\n\nOrganizations adopt tools at the speed of their legal exposure and operational friction, not at the speed of model releases. Software diffusion depends on whether risk managers can secure liability insurance and whether procurement officers have statutory authority to accept algorithmic outputs as legally binding. Bypassing evaluation bureaucracy through hardware proxies simply shifts the locus of failure from the testing lab to the courtroom, where judges and juries are left to sort out liability without a documented trail of domain-specific validation. Effective governance requires strengthening the institutional oversight bodies that review deployed behavior, matching the rigor of financial auditing and medical device clearance rather than chasing hardware shadows.",
